=> Java 21 Sequenced Collections

     -> 1. Introduction
          -> Sequenced Collections were introduced in Java 21.
          -> They provide a common API for collections that have a well-defined encounter order.
          -> Encounter order means the defined order in which elements are encountered while traversing the collection.
          -> The main purpose is to provide uniform operations for working with the first and last elements.
          -> Java 21 introduced three main sequenced interfaces:
               -> SequencedCollection<E>
               -> SequencedSet<E>
               -> SequencedMap<K,V>


     -> 2. Why Sequenced Collections Were Needed
          -> Before Java 21, several collections already maintained some form of order.
          -> Examples:
               -> ArrayList maintains positional encounter order.
               -> LinkedList maintains positional encounter order.
               -> LinkedHashSet maintains insertion-based encounter order.
               -> TreeSet maintains sorted encounter order.
               -> LinkedHashMap maintains insertion-based encounter order.
               -> TreeMap maintains sorted encounter order based on keys.

          -> However, there was no single common API for accessing and manipulating the first and last elements of all such collections.

          -> Example before Java 21 with List:
               list.get(0)
               list.get(list.size() - 1)

          -> LinkedHashSet did not have equivalent common methods such as:
               getFirst()
               getLast()

          -> Java 21 solves this by providing common sequenced interfaces and methods.


     -> 3. Encounter Order
          -> Encounter order is the defined order in which elements are encountered during traversal.

          -> Important:
               Sequenced does NOT necessarily mean insertion ordered.

          -> LinkedHashSet example:
               Insert: 30, 10, 20
               Encounter order: 30 -> 10 -> 20

          -> TreeSet example:
               Insert: 30, 10, 20
               Encounter order: 10 -> 20 -> 30

          -> HashSet:
               Does not guarantee encounter order.
               Therefore, HashSet is not a SequencedSet.

          -> Remember:
               Sequenced = well-defined encounter order.
               Encounter order can be insertion-based, positional, or sorted.


     -> 4. Basic Hierarchy
          -> Collection side:

               Iterable
                  |
               Collection
                  |
               SequencedCollection
                  |
               -------------------------
               |                       |
              List               SequencedSet
               |                       |
          ArrayList              LinkedHashSet
          LinkedList                 TreeSet

          -> List extends SequencedCollection.

          -> Therefore, ArrayList and LinkedList receive the SequencedCollection operations.

          -> SequencedSet extends both:
               Set
               SequencedCollection

          -> Therefore, a SequencedSet provides:
               Set uniqueness
               +
               Defined encounter order


     -> 5. SequencedCollection<E>
          -> SequencedCollection is an interface.

          -> It represents a Collection having a well-defined encounter order from first to last.

          -> It cannot be instantiated directly.

          -> Valid:
               SequencedCollection<String> sc = new ArrayList<>();

          -> Invalid:
               new SequencedCollection<>();

          -> Common methods include:

               getFirst()
                    -> Returns the first element.

               getLast()
                    -> Returns the last element.

               addFirst(E e)
                    -> Adds an element at the beginning.

               addLast(E e)
                    -> Adds an element at the end.

               removeFirst()
                    -> Removes and returns the first element.

               removeLast()
                    -> Removes and returns the last element.

               reversed()
                    -> Returns a reverse-ordered view of the collection.


     -> 6. SequencedCollection Does Not Mean Index
          -> A collection can have a defined encounter order without supporting indexes.

          -> Therefore:
               Order != Index

          -> Example:

               SequencedCollection<String> sc = new ArrayList<>();

          -> Allowed:
               sc.getFirst();
               sc.getLast();

          -> Not allowed:
               sc.get(0);

          -> Reason:
               The reference type is SequencedCollection.
               SequencedCollection does not declare get(int).

          -> ArrayList itself supports indexes because ArrayList implements List.


     -> 7. List and SequencedCollection
          -> List extends SequencedCollection in Java 21.

          -> Therefore:

               List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

          -> It can use List operations:
               get(index)
               set(index, value)
               add(index, value)

          -> It can also use sequenced operations:
               getFirst()
               getLast()
               addFirst()
               addLast()
               removeFirst()
               removeLast()
               reversed()

          -> Example:

               list.get(0);
               list.getFirst();

          -> Both can access the first element, but getFirst() provides a common sequenced abstraction.


     -> 8. Reference Type vs Actual Object Type
          -> Example:

               SequencedCollection<String> sc = new ArrayList<>();

          -> Reference type:
               SequencedCollection

          -> Actual object type:
               ArrayList

          -> At compile time:
               Java checks which methods are available through the reference type.

          -> Therefore:
               sc.getFirst();   // Valid
               sc.get(0);       // Compile-time error

          -> Even though the actual object is an ArrayList, get(int) is not declared by SequencedCollection.


     -> 9. Casting SequencedCollection to List
          -> Example:

               SequencedCollection<String> sc = new ArrayList<>();

               List<String> list = (List<String>) sc;

          -> Now:
               list.get(1);

          -> This works because the actual object is an ArrayList, and ArrayList implements List.

          -> The cast does not change the actual object.
          -> It only changes how the reference is viewed.

          -> Same concept with LinkedList:

               SequencedCollection<String> sc = new LinkedList<>();
               List<String> list = (List<String>) sc;

          -> This is valid because LinkedList also implements List.

          -> However:

               SequencedCollection<String> sc = new LinkedHashSet<>();
               List<String> list = (List<String>) sc;

          -> The explicit cast can compile.
          -> But it throws ClassCastException at runtime.

          -> Reason:
               The actual LinkedHashSet object is not a List.

          -> Safe checking:

               if (sc instanceof List) {
                   List<String> list = (List<String>) sc;
               }


     -> 10. SequencedSet<E>
          -> SequencedSet combines two properties:

               Set
                    -> Elements must be unique.

               SequencedCollection
                    -> Elements have a defined encounter order.

          -> Therefore:

               SequencedSet =
               Unique elements + Defined encounter order

          -> Important implementations include:
               LinkedHashSet
               TreeSet


     -> 11. LinkedHashSet as SequencedSet
          -> LinkedHashSet preserves insertion-based encounter order.

          -> Example:

               SequencedSet<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();

               set.add(30);
               set.add(10);
               set.add(20);

          -> Encounter order:

               30 -> 10 -> 20

          -> Therefore:

               set.getFirst();  // 30
               set.getLast();   // 20

          -> LinkedHashSet supports sequenced boundary operations such as:
               addFirst()
               addLast()
               getFirst()
               getLast()
               removeFirst()
               removeLast()
               reversed()


     -> 12. TreeSet as SequencedSet
          -> TreeSet also has a defined encounter order.

          -> However, its encounter order is determined by sorting.

          -> Example:

               Add:
               30, 10, 20

          -> TreeSet encounter order:

               10 -> 20 -> 30

          -> Therefore:

               getFirst() -> 10
               getLast()  -> 30

          -> reversed() gives:

               30 -> 20 -> 10

          -> Important:
               TreeSet controls element positions according to its sorting rule.

          -> Therefore:

               treeSet.addFirst(value);
               treeSet.addLast(value);

          -> These operations are not supported and throw:
               UnsupportedOperationException

          -> Even if a value would naturally become the first element after sorting,
             addFirst() is still unsupported.

          -> Correct approach:
               treeSet.add(value);

          -> TreeSet itself determines the position.


     -> 13. HashSet vs SequencedSet
          -> HashSet guarantees uniqueness.

          -> However, HashSet does not guarantee encounter order.

          -> Therefore:

               HashSet is a Set.
               HashSet is NOT a SequencedSet.

          -> LinkedHashSet:
               Unique + insertion encounter order
               -> SequencedSet

          -> TreeSet:
               Unique + sorted encounter order
               -> SequencedSet

          -> HashSet:
               Unique + no guaranteed encounter order
               -> Not SequencedSet


     -> 14. reversed()
          -> reversed() returns a reverse-ordered VIEW.

          -> It does NOT create an independent copy.

          -> It does NOT permanently reverse the original collection.

          -> Example:

               Original:
               10 -> 20 -> 30

               Reversed view:
               30 -> 20 -> 10

          -> Calling reversed() alone does not modify the original collection.

          -> The reversed view is backed by the original collection.

          -> Therefore:
               Changes to original are visible through reversed.
               Changes through reversed affect the original.


     -> 15. Operations Through a Reversed View
          -> Suppose:

               Original:
               10 -> 20 -> 30

               Reversed:
               30 -> 20 -> 10

          -> Operations correspond as follows:

               reversed.getFirst()
                    = original.getLast()

               reversed.getLast()
                    = original.getFirst()

               reversed.addFirst(X)
                    = original.addLast(X)

               reversed.addLast(X)
                    = original.addFirst(X)

               reversed.removeFirst()
                    = original.removeLast()

               reversed.removeLast()
                    = original.removeFirst()

          -> Therefore, "first" and "last" are interpreted according to the view being used.

          -> Also:

               original.reversed().reversed()

          -> gives a view with the original encounter order.


     -> 16. SequencedMap<K,V>
          -> Map is separate from Collection.

          -> Collection stores individual elements.
          -> Map stores key-value mappings.

          -> Therefore, Java 21 introduced a separate interface:

               SequencedMap<K,V>

          -> SequencedMap represents a Map whose entries have a defined encounter order.

          -> Important implementations include:
               LinkedHashMap
               TreeMap


     -> 17. Important SequencedMap Methods
          -> firstEntry()
               -> Returns the first key-value entry.
               -> Does not remove it.

          -> lastEntry()
               -> Returns the last key-value entry.
               -> Does not remove it.

          -> pollFirstEntry()
               -> Returns the first entry.
               -> Also removes it from the map.

          -> pollLastEntry()
               -> Returns the last entry.
               -> Also removes it from the map.

          -> putFirst(K key, V value)
               -> Places the mapping at the beginning when supported.

          -> putLast(K key, V value)
               -> Places the mapping at the end when supported.

          -> reversed()
               -> Returns a reverse-ordered backed view of the map.


     -> 18. LinkedHashMap as SequencedMap
          -> LinkedHashMap maintains insertion-based encounter order by default.

          -> Example:

               map.put(10, "A");
               map.put(20, "B");
               map.put(30, "C");

          -> Encounter order:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

          -> Therefore:

               map.firstEntry()
                    -> 10=A

               map.lastEntry()
                    -> 30=C


     -> 19. putFirst() and putLast() in LinkedHashMap
          -> Example:

               map:
               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

               map.putFirst(5, "X");

          -> Result:

               5=X -> 10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

          -> Then:

               map.putLast(40, "Y");

          -> Result:

               5=X -> 10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C -> 40=Y


     -> 20. Existing Key with putFirst() / putLast()
          -> Map keys must be unique.

          -> Suppose:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

          -> Execute:

               map.putFirst(20, "X");

          -> Key 20 already exists.

          -> Therefore:
               Its value changes from B to X.
               Its entry moves to the first position.

          -> Result:

               20=X -> 10=A -> 30=C

          -> Similarly:

               map.putLast(10, "Z");

          -> The existing key is updated and moved to the last position.


     -> 21. TreeMap as SequencedMap
          -> TreeMap has a defined sorted encounter order.

          -> TreeMap sorts entries according to their KEYS.

          -> Example:

               map.put(30, "C");
               map.put(10, "A");
               map.put(20, "B");

          -> Encounter order:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

          -> Therefore:

               firstEntry() -> 10=A
               lastEntry()  -> 30=C

          -> Values do not determine TreeMap sorting.
          -> Keys determine the ordering.

          -> TreeMap controls entry positions according to its sorting rule.

          -> Therefore:

               treeMap.putFirst(...)
               treeMap.putLast(...)

          -> These operations are unsupported and throw:
               UnsupportedOperationException

          -> Use normal put() instead:

               treeMap.put(5, "X");

          -> TreeMap automatically determines the correct sorted position.


     -> 22. pollFirstEntry() and pollLastEntry()
          -> firstEntry() and lastEntry() only retrieve entries.

          -> pollFirstEntry() and pollLastEntry() retrieve AND remove entries.

          -> Example:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

               Map.Entry<Integer,String> e =
                   map.pollFirstEntry();

          -> e:
               10=A

          -> Remaining map:

               20=B -> 30=C

          -> If:

               map.pollLastEntry();

          -> The last entry is returned and removed.


     -> 23. reversed() with SequencedMap
          -> SequencedMap.reversed() returns a reverse-ordered backed view.

          -> Example:

               Original:
               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

               Reversed:
               30=C -> 20=B -> 10=A

          -> Calling reversed() alone does NOT modify the original map.

          -> Both views refer to the same underlying map.

          -> Therefore:
               Modification through original is visible in reversed.
               Modification through reversed affects original.


     -> 24. SequencedMap Reversed-View Operations
          -> Operations correspond as follows:

               reversed.firstEntry()
                    = original.lastEntry()

               reversed.lastEntry()
                    = original.firstEntry()

               reversed.putFirst(K,V)
                    = original.putLast(K,V)

               reversed.putLast(K,V)
                    = original.putFirst(K,V)

               reversed.pollFirstEntry()
                    = original.pollLastEntry()

               reversed.pollLastEntry()
                    = original.pollFirstEntry()

          -> Example:

               Original:
               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

               Reversed:
               30=C -> 20=B -> 10=A

          -> Execute:

               reversed.putFirst(40, "D");

          -> Reversed becomes:

               40=D -> 30=C -> 20=B -> 10=A

          -> Original becomes:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C -> 40=D


     -> 25. sequencedKeySet()
          -> SequencedMap provides:

               sequencedKeySet()

          -> It returns:

               SequencedSet<K>

          -> Example map:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

          -> sequencedKeySet():

               10 -> 20 -> 30

          -> It is a SequencedSet because:
               Map keys must be unique.
               Keys follow the map's encounter order.

          -> Therefore:

               Unique keys + Defined encounter order
               = SequencedSet<K>


     -> 26. sequencedValues()
          -> SequencedMap provides:

               sequencedValues()

          -> It returns:

               SequencedCollection<V>

          -> Example:

               10=A
               20=A
               30=B

          -> Values:

               A -> A -> B

          -> Map values can contain duplicates.

          -> Therefore, values cannot generally be represented as a Set.

          -> Hence:

               sequencedValues()
               -> SequencedCollection<V>


     -> 27. sequencedEntrySet()
          -> SequencedMap provides:

               sequencedEntrySet()

          -> It returns:

               SequencedSet<Map.Entry<K,V>>

          -> Each element represents one key-value mapping.

          -> Example:

               10=A
               20=B
               30=C

          -> Entries:

               10=A -> 20=B -> 30=C

          -> Map entries form a Set because mappings are identified through unique keys.

          -> Since the entries also follow the map's defined encounter order:

               sequencedEntrySet()
               -> SequencedSet<Map.Entry<K,V>>


     -> 28. Key, Value and Entry Views - Quick Comparison
          -> sequencedKeySet()
               -> SequencedSet<K>
               -> Keys are unique.
               -> Maintains map encounter order.

          -> sequencedValues()
               -> SequencedCollection<V>
               -> Values may contain duplicates.
               -> Maintains map encounter order.

          -> sequencedEntrySet()
               -> SequencedSet<Map.Entry<K,V>>
               -> Entries form a set.
               -> Maintains map encounter order.


     -> 29. Important Difference - Insertion Order vs Sorted Order
          -> LinkedHashSet:
               Encounter order is based on insertion/explicit sequencing.

          -> TreeSet:
               Encounter order is based on sorted elements.

          -> LinkedHashMap:
               Encounter order is based on insertion/explicit sequencing.

          -> TreeMap:
               Encounter order is based on sorted keys.

          -> Therefore:

               Sequenced does NOT mean insertion ordered.

          -> Sequenced only guarantees:
               A well-defined first-to-last encounter order.


     -> 30. Important OCP / Interview Points
          -> SequencedCollection was introduced in Java 21.

          -> SequencedCollection represents collections with a defined encounter order.

          -> Order does not imply index support.

          -> List extends SequencedCollection.

          -> SequencedSet combines uniqueness with defined encounter order.

          -> LinkedHashSet uses insertion-based encounter order.

          -> TreeSet uses sorted encounter order.

          -> HashSet does not implement SequencedSet because it does not guarantee encounter order.

          -> TreeSet addFirst() and addLast() are unsupported.

          -> SequencedMap is separate because Map is not a Collection.

          -> LinkedHashMap supports explicit first/last positioning.

          -> TreeMap encounter order is determined by sorted keys.

          -> TreeMap putFirst() and putLast() are unsupported.

          -> firstEntry()/lastEntry() retrieve entries without removing them.

          -> pollFirstEntry()/pollLastEntry() retrieve and remove entries.

          -> reversed() returns a backed reverse-order view, not an independent copy.

          -> Modifying a reversed view modifies the underlying collection/map.

          -> sequencedKeySet() returns SequencedSet because keys are unique.

          -> sequencedValues() returns SequencedCollection because values can repeat.

          -> sequencedEntrySet() returns SequencedSet of Map.Entry objects.


     -> 31. Final Quick Revision
          -> SequencedCollection
               -> Defined encounter order
               -> getFirst()
               -> getLast()
               -> addFirst()
               -> addLast()
               -> removeFirst()
               -> removeLast()
               -> reversed()

          -> SequencedSet
               -> Set + SequencedCollection
               -> Unique elements
               -> Defined encounter order
               -> LinkedHashSet
               -> TreeSet

          -> SequencedMap
               -> Defined encounter order of mappings
               -> firstEntry()
               -> lastEntry()
               -> putFirst()
               -> putLast()
               -> pollFirstEntry()
               -> pollLastEntry()
               -> reversed()
               -> sequencedKeySet()
               -> sequencedValues()
               -> sequencedEntrySet()

          -> Core Memory Rule
               SequencedCollection = ordered elements
               SequencedSet        = unique + ordered elements
               SequencedMap        = ordered key-value mappings

          -> Most Important Concept
               Sequenced means "well-defined encounter order."
               It does NOT mean "insertion order."
               It does NOT mean "index-based."